  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nIsaias is now considered a Post-Tropical Cyclone. It's centered \nover AL at the time of this discussion. The center will pass over\nor just to our west through tonight. It is then expected to track\ninto the Virginia's tomorrow. Much like a mid-latitude cyclone \ntypically seen in the colder season, wrap-around from the system \nwill continue to bring precipitation over the higher terrain into \nearly Monday.\n\nThe Flood Watch was dropped much earlier than forecast due to much\nlighter rainfall rates and general accumulated precipitation thus\nfar. Heaviest rainfall amounts have been across the southern \nportion of our forecast area immediately bordering GA and SW NC.\nAreas that may possibly see close to an inch or more of rain to\ncome will be the higher elevations in SW VA and along upslope \nparts of the Southern Appalachians.\n\nWind has been a complicating element of this storm. At least from\nobservations we do have from mountainous terrain, nothing has come\nclose to High Wind Warning for any areas currently under that\nhazard. Winds didn't increase late last night into this morning\nlike forecast models had shown. Most areas have recorded gusts\nbetween 25 and up to 40 mph since early this morning; very isolated\ngusts over 40. According to high-res models, stronger gusts will \nmove out of much of the valley and plateau as we approach the\nevening. Wind gusts will confine more to the Southern Appalachians\nand northeast parts of the forecast area including, NE TN and SW \nVA. Higher elevation gusts peak sometime tonight before decreasing\ninto tomorrow morning. It's possible the Wind Advisory and High\nWind Warning currently out, become refined and/or cancelled \nearlier than 8 am tomorrow.\n\nFollowing the phasing of the closed upper low eventually \ndeparting to our east, upper level ridging will move in from the \nwest the beginning of the new work-week. This will dry things out \nfor us as we get later into Monday and especially through at least\nearly Wednesday. Upper 80s to near 90 will be possible Wednesday \nafternoon. Another trough and possible shortwave may impact the \nregion towards the end of the week/weekend, but deterministic runs\ndiffer greatly on the synoptic pattern.",
